# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Environment variables — DOCUMENTATION for developers
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Vite loads env files at BUILD/DEV time based on the mode, then merges
# them. Priority per variable = the LAST file loaded wins.
#
#   .env                → ALWAYS loaded (base values; put production here)
#   .env.development    → ONLY on `npm run dev` (mode=development) → overrides .env
#   .env.production     → ONLY on `npm run build` (mode=production) → overrides .env
#
# Which command uses which backend:
#
#   npm run dev        → .env + .env.development        → DEV backend
#                        (https://dev.dynamicsroute.tech)
#   npm run build      → .env (+ .env.production if any) → PROD backend
#                        (https://app.dynamicsroute.tech)
#
# Rules:
#   • Only `.env.example` is committed. `.env`, `.env.development`,
#     `.env.production` are all gitignored — each developer creates
#     them locally (copy this example).
#   • `.env.production` is OPTIONAL: only needed when a production build
#     must NOT use the values in `.env` (e.g. a staging/prod-like build).
#     Default = `.env` already holds production values → skip it.
#   • Editing an env file requires restarting `npm run dev` or a rebuild —
#     Vite bakes the values into the bundle at compile time.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy this file to `.env` (production) and `.env.development` (dev),
# replacing the placeholder values below.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# API base URL
VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://your-api-domain.com

# WebSocket URL
VITE_WEBSOCKET_URL=https://your-api-domain.com

# JWT token expiration time in days
VITE_JWT_EXPIRATION_DAYS=7

# Application debug mode
VITE_APP_DEBUG=false